      "weight_variable_name": null,
      "weight_fields_interpretation": "The study uses PISA 2022 Spanish student microdata and constructs an analytic dataset with 74 student-context variables, then applies preprocessing and dimensionality reduction before clustering and subsequent cluster classification. The manuscript does not report the use of PISA sampling/student weights or any replicate-weight scheme, and it does not name any weight variable (e.g., TOTWGT or W_FSTUWT). Because the core predictive step uses ML models (k-means for clustering and supervised classification with SHAP-based interpretability), the paper’s weighting strategy is unclear; if weights were omitted, this would imply estimates are not fully adjusted for PISA’s complex sampling design. Analysts using the PISA database should apply the official PISA student weights and replicate weights when estimating population-representative relationships, and should document whether the ML pipeline was run with or without weight-aware resampling."
    },
    "plausible_values_handling": "not_applicable",
    "missing_data_handling": "not_reported",
    "handling_not_reported_explanation": "The dependent variables in the supervised stage are student-cluster membership labels derived from clustering, rather than cognitive achievement scores expressed as PISA plausible values. As a result, plausible-value combining rules are not directly applicable to the target used for prediction. The manuscript reports variable-level missingness rates for many inputs but does not specify the missing-data handling method used in the preprocessing stage (e.g., listwise deletion, imputation type), which is a reporting gap because missingness can affect both dimensionality reduction and clustering/classification stability.",
    "sample_details": {
      "total_students": 30800,
      "countries": [
        {
          "country_code": "ESP",
          "n_students": 30800
        }
      ],
      "sample_filtering_criteria": "Used the entire PISA 2022 Spanish student sample (n = 30,800) and selected 74 student contextual variables spanning socio-economic/cultural, psychological, cognitive/academic, and ICT domains. The pipeline includes a data pre-processing stage intended to ensure data completeness and quality, but the specific inclusion/exclusion rules (e.g., complete-case threshold, imputation vs. deletion) are not explicitly stated; therefore the analytic sample is described as the full available sample after the authors’ preprocessing."
    },
